unctions (including trigonometry) and become acquainted with limits and derivatives in calculus. Upon completing the course, students will be prepared for Mathematical Foundations III , the final stepping stone in preparing for university-level math." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046647 #edb/ref 1009 "Master the algebra of advanced functions including quadratics, logarithms, and trigonometry. Dive deep into the theory of polynomials, learn the basics of limits, derivatives, and integrals from calculus, and explore a variety of concepts from higher math including complex numbers, vectors, probability, and statistics." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046647 #edb/ref 1012 111 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046647 #edb/ref 1013 "MF2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046647 #edb/ref 1014 #uuid "b5d8b420-892a-5b6a-8150-ba3c70c4d779" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048142 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048166 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048142 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186133932 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048142 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186133933 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048142 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186133934 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048142 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186133935 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048142 #edb/ref 1031 "Translating Between Explicit and Recursive Formulas for Geometric Sequences" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048142 #edb/ref 1033 2215 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048142 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "82d65d50-f769-531a-8e21-07640e0f163f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048166 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048386 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048166 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050254 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048166 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050598 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048166 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050925 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048166 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186078436 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048166 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186078437 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048166 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186078564 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048166 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186078565 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048166 #edb/ref 1031 "Determining Indexes of Terms in Geometric Sequences" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048166 #edb/ref 1033 685 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048166 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "84e2509e-1fbc-5c17-a4bf-5c3aa6194b5d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048829 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050067 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048829 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186119950 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048829 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186119951 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048829 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186119952 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048829 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186119953 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048829 #edb/ref 1031 "The Recursive Formula for a Geometric Sequence" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048829 #edb/ref 1033 1818 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048829 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "c4140dd8-d457-5cdb-8b51-1b7b952642cd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050067 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048142 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050067 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049076 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050067 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049083 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050067 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050155 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050067 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050598 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050067 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186078558 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050067 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186078559 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050067 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186078560 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050067 #edb/ref 1031 "The Nth Term of a Geometric Sequence" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050067 #edb/ref 1033 680 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050067 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "2a8497f4-0ed3-5e71-a11d-456426f348f7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133536 #edb/ref 1069 "e-1512" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133536 #edb/ref 1071 "The formula for the $n$th term of a geometric sequence is $f(n) =\\dfrac{6}{(-2)^n}$ for $n\\geq 1$. What is the recursive formula for this sequence?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133536 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5537647b-d722-5000-8445-b2743cc1f1f8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133536 #edb/ref 1074 "The recursive formula for a geometric sequence is\n\n$$\nf(n+1) = r \\cdot f(n)\n$$\n\nTo complete the formula, we must determine the common ratio $r$ and state the first term $a_1$. The first two terms are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(1) &= (6)/((-2)^{1}) = (6)/((-2)) =-3, \\\\\nf(2) &= (6)/((-2)^{2}) = \\frac{6}{4} = \\frac{3}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr = (f(2))/(f(1)) = ((\\frac{3}{2}))/((-3)) =-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the recursive formula for the geometric sequence is\n\n$$\nf(n+1) = -\\dfrac{1}{2} f(n), \\qquad f(1) = -3, \\qquad n \\geq 1\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133537 #edb/ref 1069 "e-1513" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133537 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the explicit formula for the $n$th term of the geometric sequence\n$a_{n+1} = 3a_n,\\, \\qquad a_1 = 1,\\, \\qquad n\\geq 1$?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133537 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "19d183f9-cf05-5477-8da9-642e2fd8e03b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133537 #edb/ref 1074 "The explicit formula for the $n$th term of a geometric sequence is\n\n$$\na_n = a_1 \\cdot r^{n-1}, \\qquad n \\geq 1\n$$\n\nSo, we must determine the first term $a_1$ and the common ratio $r$.\n\nWe are given that the first term is $a_1=1$. To find the common ratio, we compare the given sequence with the general recursive formula for a geometric sequence:\n\n$$\na_{n+1} = r \\cdot a_n\n$$\n\nFrom the above, we see that $r = 3$.\n\nSubstituting $a_1 = 1$ and $r = 3$ into the formula for the $n$th term, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} a_n &= a_1 \\cdot r^{n-1} \\\\[5pt] &= 1 \\cdot 3^{n-1} \\\\[5pt] &= 3^{n-1}. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\na_n=3^{n-1}, \\quad n \\geq 1\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133550 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4237" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133550 #edb/ref 1071 "The formula for the $n$th term of a geometric sequence is $a_n = 8 \\cdot 3^n$ for $n\\geq 1$. What is the recursive formula for this sequence?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133550 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6aa991c4-28e3-51fc-9642-19fa9346202d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133550 #edb/ref 1074 "The recursive formula for a geometric sequence is\n\n$$\na_{n+1} = r \\cdot a_n\n$$\n\nTo complete the formula, we must determine the common ratio $r$ and state the first term $a_1$. The first two terms are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{1} &= 8 \\cdot 3^{1} = 8 \\cdot 3 = 24, \\\\\na_{2} &= 8 \\cdot 3^{2} = 8 \\cdot 9 = 72\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr = \\frac{a_{2}}{a_{1}} = \\frac{72}{24} = 3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the recursive formula for the geometric sequence is\n\n$$\na_{n+1} = 3 a_n, \\quad a_1 = 24, \\quad n \\geq 1\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133551 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4238" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133551 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the explicit formula for the $n$th term of the sequence\n$f(n+1) =-\\dfrac{f(n)}{3},\\, \\quad f(1) = {\\color{blue}9},\\, \\quad n\\geq 1$?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133551 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5027a265-fd62-574b-b6fa-2bacf9de7304" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133551 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's rewrite our recursive formula as follows:\n\n$$\nf(n + 1) = - (f(n))/(3) =-\\frac{1}{3}f(n)\n$$\n\nThe explicit formula for the $n$th term of a geometric sequence is\n\n$$\nf(n) =f(1) \\cdot r^{n-1}, \\quad n \\geq 1\n$$\n\nSo, we must determine the first term $f(1)$ and the common ratio $r$.\n\nWe are given that the first term is\n\n$$\nf(1)=9\n$$\n\nTo find the common ratio, we compare the given sequence with the general recursive formula for a geometric sequence:\n\n$$\nf(n+1) = {\\color{red}r} \\cdot f(n)\n$$\n\nFrom the above, we see that\n\n$$\nr = -\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\nf(1) = 9\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nr = -\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\ninto the formula for the $n$th term, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} f(n) &= f(1) \\cdot r^{n-1} \\\\[5pt] &= 9 \\cdot \\left(-\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^{n-1}. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify further by manipulating the exponents as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(n) &= 9 \\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3})^{n - 1} \\\\\n&= 9 \\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3})^{-1} \\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3})^{n} \\\\\n&= 9 \\cdot (-\\frac{3}{1}) \\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3})^{n} \\\\\n&=-27 \\cdot (1)/((-3)^{n}) \\\\\n&= - (27)/((-3)^{n})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\nf(n)=-\\dfrac{27}{(-3)^n},\\quad n\\geq 1\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133932 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048829 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133932 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134173 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133932 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134445 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133932 #edb/ref 1039 "Translating From the Explicit to the Recursive Formula" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133932 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186133550 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133932 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "f258f110-e541-5edf-8050-54d03177e499" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133933 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050067 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133933 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134174 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133933 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134175 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133933 #edb/ref 1039 "Translating From the Explicit to the Recursive Formula Using Function Notation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133933 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186133536 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133933 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "6af17da8-272b-55fb-8a5d-5857bd41051e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133934 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134176 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133934 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134444 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133934 #edb/ref 1039 "Translating From the Recursive to the Explicit Formula" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133934 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186133537 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133934 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "ac7a8723-02b7-5467-98f7-530ba8239c4f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133935 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134179 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133935 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134478 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133935 #edb/ref 1039 "Translating From the Recursive to the Explicit Formula Using Function Notation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133935 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186133551 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133935 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "6a910df9-7b4a-5892-a084-52d8b5dcd086" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134050 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134940 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134050 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134941 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134050 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134942 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134050 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134943 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134050 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134944 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134050 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134945 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134050 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134050 #edb/ref 1045 "Translating Between Explicit and Recursive Formulas for Geometric Sequences" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134050 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186134940 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134050 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048142 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134050 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "a09fce56-8cba-5c04-b594-13a87263e94e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134940 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134941 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134940 #edb/ref 1061 459983 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134940 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d53098fa-92a8-5d13-9bb6-afc94451163e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134940 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135016 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134941 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134942 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134941 #edb/ref 1061 459984 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134941 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "28a0a6a2-8445-5dbf-bab4-94273d8bd5a2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134941 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133932 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134942 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134943 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134942 #edb/ref 1061 459987 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134942 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "842bc2ad-3287-51df-b12a-9c624c1f5178" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134942 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133933 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134943 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134944 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134943 #edb/ref 1061 459990 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134943 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5d1a36ce-aed5-518c-95d0-8d09a832322f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134943 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135017 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134944 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134945 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134944 #edb/ref 1061 459991 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134944 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d3301f00-0418-594d-8113-7d7d6804a694" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134944 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133934 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134945 #edb/ref 1061 459994 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134945 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3c4a6c1b-5e7f-529b-9fd1-f8e9ae72b8ed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134945 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133935 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135016 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that we're given the following **explicit formula** for a geometric sequence:\n\n$$\na_n = 2\\cdot 5^n, \\qquad n\\geq 1\n$$\n\nWe can write this geometric sequence using a recursive formula. Recall that the recursive formula for *any* geometric sequence is\n\n$$\na_{n+1} = r\\cdot a_n\n$$\n\nTo convert from the explicit formula to the recursive formula, we just need to work out the first term and common ratio. The first two terms are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{1} &= 2 \\cdot 5^{1} = 10 \\\\\na_{2} &= 2 \\cdot 5^{2} = 50\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr = \\frac{a_{2}}{a_{1}} = \\frac{50}{10} = 5\n$$\n\nSo, the first term is\n\n$$\na_1={\\color{blue}10}\n$$\n\nand the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr={\\color{red}5}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the recursive formula is as follows:\n\n$$\na_{n+1} = {\\color{red}5} \\cdot a_n, \\qquad a_1 = {\\color{blue}10}, \\qquad n \\geq 1\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135016 #edb/ref 1065 1670 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135016 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "3cb2e1b6-42f6-5508-aa68-1085dd64e89c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135016 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135017 #edb/ref 1064 "It's often more useful to translate from the recursive formula to an explicit one. So how do we find the explicit formula for the geometric sequence defined recursively as\n\n$$\na_{n+1} ={\\color{red}\\dfrac 1 4} a_n,\\, \\qquad a_1 = {\\color{blue}2},\\, \\qquad n\\geq 1\n$$\n\nThe explicit formula for a geometric sequence is\n\n$$\na_n = a_1 \\cdot r^{n-1}, \\quad n \\geq 1\n$$\n\nso we just need to determine the first term $a_1$ and the common ratio $r$.\n\nWe are given that the first term is\n\n$$\na_1={\\color{blue}2}\n$$\n\nTo find the common ratio, we compare the given sequence with the general recursive formula for a geometric sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n + 1} &= r \\cdot a_{n} \\\\\na_{n + 1} &= \\frac{1}{4}a_{n}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the above, we see that\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}r} = {\\color{red}\\dfrac 1 4}\n$$\n\nNow we substitute\n\n$$\na_1={\\color{blue}2}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nr={\\color{red}\\dfrac{1}{4}}\n$$\n\ninto the formula for the $n$th term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} a_n &= a_1 \\cdot r^{n-1} \\\\ &= {\\color{blue}2} \\cdot \\left({\\color{red}\\dfrac{1}{4}} \\right)^{n-1} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\na_n=2 \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac{1}{4} \\right)^{n-1}, \\quad n \\geq 1\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135017 #edb/ref 1065 1671 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135017 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "d326c47a-d9db-5669-be98-4bee64971fd7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135017 #edb/ref 1067 "Translating From the Recursive to the Explicit Formula" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
